dering the game history, the previous player played T2, T4, etc. Not sure. \\n\\nMaybe the best is to discard a tile that is part of a sequence I can still form. For example, if I discard T9, I still have T7 and T8, so I can form T7, T8, T9 if I draw a T7 or T8. Wait, I already have T7 (two) and T8. If I discard T9, I have T7, T8, and need T9 to complete the sequence. But I already have T9 in hand. Wait, no, the user's hand has T9:1. So if I discard T9, I lose that tile. But I have T7, T8, and T9. If I discard T9, I can still form a sequence with T7, T8, and another T9 if I draw. But since I have one T9, discarding it might not be ideal. \\n\\nAlternatively, if I keep T9, I have the full sequence T7, T8, T9. That's a good sequence. \\n\\nSo maybe it's better to discard a tile that isn't part of a sequence. Like B3, F4, or J1. \\n\\nBetween those, B3 is a suited tile. The player across has B1, B2, B9. Maybe they don't need B3. \\n\\nDiscarding B3 seems safe. \\n\\nSo the answer would be Play B3.\\n\", \"output\": \"Evaluation: The safest tile to discard is B3, as it is a single tile in the hand with multiple remaining in the wall and is less likely to be needed by others for melds.  \\nReason: B3 has 4 remaining tiles in the unshown, and no clear indication others need it. Discarding it avoids giving opponents a chance to form a Pung or complete a sequence.  \\nAnswer: Play B3\"}"}
